---
import { UPGRADE_PRICE_CENTS } from '@boombapbot/contracts';
import { SitePage } from '@boombapbot/editor';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const UPGRADE_PRICE = `${UPGRADE_PRICE_CENTS / 100} dollars`;
---

<Base
  title="Policies · boombapbot"
  description="What boombapbot keeps, how the upgrade and the shop work, the terms it is offered on, and what it is and is not."
>
  <SitePage client:load baseUrl={base} current="policies">
    <h1>Policies</h1>
    <p class="sb-lede">
      This page is short because the site does very little with you.
    </p>

    <Section title="Privacy">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You don't need an account.</strong> The machine works the same without one, and without one the
          site does not know who you are.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you make an account, I keep three things.</strong> Your email address. A scrambled form of your
          password that I can't read. And for each time you log in: when it was, the IP address it came from and
          which browser it was, so you stay logged in and I can tell you from someone guessing. Beats are kept only
          if you save them to it, as set out below.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you upload a picture, I keep a small copy of it.</strong> Every account starts with a drawing of
          a robot, made from the account itself. Upload a picture of your own and your browser cuts it to a small
          square first; only that square is sent and kept, never the file you picked. It is shown on the account
          button, and anybody with its address could see it, as with any picture on a page. Removing it deletes
          it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>I email you only when you ask.</strong> Once to confirm the address, and again if you ask to
          reset your password. Buying the upgrade sends its key, as set out below. Nothing else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One cookie.</strong> Logging in sets a cookie that keeps you logged in. Logging out removes it.
          There are no advertising or tracking cookies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your work stays in your browser unless you save it to your account.</strong> Patterns and knob
          positions are kept in this browser's storage on your device. With no account, or with one you never
          save a beat to, they are not sent anywhere. Clearing the site's data in your browser removes them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you save a beat to your account, I keep that beat.</strong> Pressing save in the account
          window sends a copy of the project to the database: its name, its patterns and knob positions, how
          large it is and when it was saved. Nothing goes there until you press save, and an account holds up to 20
          beats. A saved beat is tied to your account and the site only hands it back while you are logged in.
          Deleting one there removes it from the database, and the copy in your browser stays where it is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No sound is uploaded.</strong> Sound is made on your device, and exports are files your browser
          saves for you. A saved beat is steps and settings, not audio.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No analytics, no ads, no third-party scripts.</strong> Three companies do the plumbing: Vercel
          hosts the site and keeps ordinary request logs, Neon runs the database the accounts and saved beats live in, and Resend
          delivers the emails. Buying the upgrade or from the shop brings in Stripe, and the shop brings in
          Printful, both set out below.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deleting an account.</strong> The account window has a delete button. It asks for your password,
          then removes the account, its log-ins, its picture and every beat saved in it straight away. Orders you placed are
          kept as records, no longer tied to an account. A license you bought is kept too, and its key still
          works.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The upgrade">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What it is.</strong> One payment of {UPGRADE_PRICE}, once. It adds six effects to the machine: a
          crusher, tape, a pump, a filter, vinyl and a phaser. There is no subscription and nothing renews. The
          machine, its eight sounds and its first four effects stay free.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What you get.</strong> A license key, sent to the email address you give when you pay. You don't
          need an account to buy it. The key is yours to use on as many of your own devices as you like, and I
          don't count them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Refunds.</strong> Ask within 30 days and I'll give you the {UPGRADE_PRICE} back, whatever the
          reason. After a refund the key stops unlocking anything new.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What you pay.</strong> The price is in US dollars. I don't collect sales tax or VAT on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who takes the money.</strong> Stripe does, on its own page. Your card details go to Stripe and
          never reach this site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What a license keeps.</strong> The email address the key was sent to, the key, when you bought
          it and the reference Stripe gives the payment. If you were logged in, or you enter the key in an
          account, the license is tied to that account. Deleting the account unties it and keeps the license, so
          the key still works.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you lose the key.</strong> Ask in the upgrade window and I'll send it again to the address
          that bought it. To stop that being used to pester anyone, I count how often an address is asked about
          and how often a visitor asks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What stays on your device.</strong> Entering a key, or logging in to an upgraded account, puts a
          small license file on the device. The machine checks that file itself, without asking the site, so
          the upgrade works with no connection. The file holds the license's number, when it was issued and what it
          unlocks. It does not hold your name or your email address.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A beat made with the upgrade opens for anyone.</strong> Without the upgrade it plays with that
          effect off, and says so.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The shop">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Returns and refunds.</strong> Everything in the shop is printed to order when you buy it, so I
          can't take returns for a wrong size or a change of mind. If something arrives damaged or misprinted, or
          doesn't arrive, tell me within 30 days, with a photo if there's something to show, and I'll send a
          replacement or refund you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What you pay.</strong> Prices are in US dollars. You pay for the items and for the delivery you
          choose, which is priced for your address. I don't collect sales tax or VAT. If your country charges
          customs or import duty on a parcel, that is yours to pay when it arrives.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who takes the money.</strong> Stripe does, on its own page. Your card details go to Stripe and
          never reach this site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who makes and sends it.</strong> Printful prints each order and posts it. To do that it gets
          your name, delivery address and email address. It also gets the address before you pay, to price the
          delivery.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What an order keeps.</strong> Your name, email address and delivery address, what you ordered
          and what it cost, and later the parcel's tracking number. If you were logged in, the order is tied to
          your account. The order is written down when you go to pay, so it is kept even if you turn back at
          Stripe's page.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Terms">
      <ul>
        <li>What you make is yours. Use your exports however you like.</li>
        <li>
          The site is offered as it is, with no warranty. It is a preview: things may change or break, and a saved
          beat may stop opening. Export the project file to keep a copy.
        </li>
        <li>Do not use the site to harm it or anyone else.</li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Disclosures">
      <ul>
        <li>
          boombapbot is an independent project. Its machine, РИТМ-12, is invented, and so is the maker's name on
          its panel. It copies no real machine.
        </li>
        <li>The sounds are synthesised here. They are not samples or recordings of any hardware.</li>
        <li>The picture of the machine is original artwork.</li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Contact">
      <p>
        Questions come to me at <a href="https://mm.coffee" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">mm.coffee</a>.
      </p>
    </Section>
  </SitePage>
</Base>
